00:47:13,780 --> 00:47:14,180
Okay.

973
00:47:14,400 --> 00:47:17,600
That's a problem though, I think, if that is the case.

974
00:47:17,660 --> 00:47:18,620
Is that truly the case?

975
00:47:18,620 --> 00:47:21,960
I mean, that's not what you're paying for.

976
00:47:22,420 --> 00:47:24,840
You know, you're not paying them to tell you how to run your...

977
00:47:24,840 --> 00:47:27,780
Like they can give you advice, but it would be unsolicited, right?

978
00:47:27,860 --> 00:47:29,620
And so that is like...

979
00:47:29,620 --> 00:47:36,880
Like I think it would be better if they were able to like meaningfully give advice to companies.

980
00:47:36,880 --> 00:47:43,440
But I think it's not really their place to be doing that because that's not what they're being paid for.

981
00:47:44,120 --> 00:47:50,960
And so not only is it an overstep of their services, but it's like not really their concern.

982
00:47:51,020 --> 00:47:59,560
Like their concern is to provide an excellent BugBounty platform that helps the company communicate with researchers and receive vulnerabilities and all that kind of stuff.

983
00:47:59,560 --> 00:48:05,480
Yeah, but you can't provide an excellent BugBounty platform without it being a two-way street, right?

984
00:48:05,520 --> 00:48:11,560
So if you're not ensuring that the program is providing a quality experience to the hackers, then you can't...

985
00:48:12,080 --> 00:48:15,840
Then the platform is going down, right?

986
00:48:15,860 --> 00:48:17,760
But again, like what is HackerOne going to do?

987
00:48:17,880 --> 00:48:25,220
Say like, oh, in order to sign this contract, you have to provide us that, you know, you're going to adjust your security SLAs and do all the...

988
00:48:25,220 --> 00:48:26,880
Like these things would be blockers that...

989
00:48:26,880 --> 00:48:27,380
That'd be great.

990
00:48:27,380 --> 00:48:29,420
I mean, they would just lose business, right?

991
00:48:29,420 --> 00:48:29,800
That's true.

992
00:48:30,220 --> 00:48:31,720
And it's a company, you know?

993
00:48:31,940 --> 00:48:32,060
Yeah.

994
00:48:32,100 --> 00:48:35,140
Yeah, the company will be like, okay, we're just going to go to BugCrow who doesn't require that.

995
00:48:35,580 --> 00:48:35,800
Yeah.

996
00:48:35,920 --> 00:48:37,120
Or like whatever, right?

997
00:48:37,180 --> 00:48:49,260
Like the reality is that I think that would be too much of a blocker when the goal is to just get companies to adopt security more by having a BugBounty program and having a way to receive vulnerability reports.

998
00:48:49,260 --> 00:48:57,600
And to make it a requirement that they have to like vamp up and buff their whole security program in order to receive reports.

999
00:48:57,920 --> 00:48:58,980
That's really difficult.

1000
00:48:59,320 --> 00:49:00,420
That's a really hard sell.

1001
00:49:00,420 --> 00:49:01,140
Yeah.

1002
00:49:01,140 --> 00:49:02,220
I don't know, man.

1003
00:49:02,220 --> 00:49:03,900
I would like to see that sell.

1004
00:49:03,900 --> 00:49:05,620
And maybe this is a...

1005
00:49:05,620 --> 00:49:18,280
Maybe something that HackerOne or BugCrowder and TGD could run with and say like, these are HackerOne vetted programs or something like that where...

1006
00:49:18,280 --> 00:49:26,300
You know, and they have some sort of extra symbol, you know, sort of like they have for some of the other things that I'm not even sure I can mention exist in HackerOne.

1007
00:49:26,820 --> 00:49:28,180
Yeah, I mean, I mentioned this.

1008
00:49:28,620 --> 00:49:34,100
I actually had a discussion with them, not related to work, from like a hacker side.

1009
00:49:34,440 --> 00:49:36,120
I was asked as a hacker to go speak with...

1010
00:49:36,120 --> 00:49:36,980
Whoa, whoa, whoa, Joel.

1011
00:49:37,160 --> 00:49:38,360
You're not a hacker today.

1012
00:49:39,060 --> 00:49:39,620
I'm sorry.

1013
00:49:39,840 --> 00:49:40,560
Continue, continue.

1014
00:49:40,560 --> 00:49:54,020
But like one of the things that I suggested was like, if you have some knowledge about the tech stack that's being used, why not provide security recommendations for that tech stack, right?

1015
00:49:54,080 --> 00:50:03,940
If you know about stuff or you know about trends, like that's an easy win for your customer to like give them extra value for the product.

1016
00:50:04,440 --> 00:50:06,940
And maybe you later turn that into an extra paid feature.

1017
00:50:07,120 --> 00:50:07,440
I don't know.

1018
00:50:07,560 --> 00:50:07,960
I don't care.

1019
00:50:07,960 --> 00:50:12,080
But like, I think that there are certainly things that they could do there.

1020
00:50:12,320 --> 00:50:18,020
That's not quite going as far as saying, here's what you should have for your SLAs and all that kind of stuff.

1021
00:50:18,080 --> 00:50:18,960
Because I think that is...

1022
00:50:19,820 --> 00:50:20,740
I mean...

1023
00:50:20,740 --> 00:50:23,220
That's a very intense decision as well.

1024
00:50:23,340 --> 00:50:24,260
I acknowledge.

1025
00:50:24,580 --> 00:50:29,640
And in order to hit those SLAs, you may even have to bring on additional developers, right?

1026
00:50:29,720 --> 00:50:33,740
Which is a, you know, six-figure a year at least decision, you know?

1027
00:50:33,740 --> 00:50:36,600
And so there is a lot of nuance to that.

1028
00:50:36,600 --> 00:50:40,080
And I guess I can argue for the ideal from the side as the hacker.

1029
00:50:40,380 --> 00:50:49,100
But I just feel like a bug bounty program is a more mature security decision, you know?

1030
00:50:49,340 --> 00:50:51,740
And a VDP, everyone should have a VDP.

1031
00:50:51,900 --> 00:50:53,620
You can be throwing VDPs out left and right.

1032
00:50:53,980 --> 00:50:59,960
But I think a bug bounty program is a little bit more of a mature security program decision.

1033
00:50:59,960 --> 00:51:02,820
And by that point, you should have a grip on this.

1034
00:51:03,460 --> 00:51:04,440
Yes, I totally agree.

1035
00:51:04,580 --> 00:51:10,520
I think HackerOne would enthusiastically disagree with me.

1036
00:51:12,580 --> 00:51:18,600
Just based on the fact that it's their whole business to create and start more programs.

1037
00:51:18,880 --> 00:51:22,500
That's why we can say whatever the heck we want on critical thinking.

1038
00:51:22,500 --> 00:51:24,360
That's good.

1039
00:51:24,700 --> 00:51:24,960
All right.

1040
00:51:26,040 --> 00:51:26,820
So, all right.

1041
00:51:26,940 --> 00:51:28,780
I have CVSS as the next topic.

1042
00:51:29,820 --> 00:51:31,400
Little bit of a loaded topic.

1043
00:51:33,880 --> 00:51:34,540
What do you think?

1044
00:51:34,640 --> 00:51:35,840
Skip it and move on to budget?

1045
00:51:36,040 --> 00:51:36,980
Or do we want to...

1046
00:51:36,980 --> 00:51:39,740
Would you like me to yell at you a little bit here?

1047
00:51:40,100 --> 00:51:40,760
I mean, sure.

1048
00:51:41,120 --> 00:51:42,540
Like, here's my take.

1049
00:51:42,740 --> 00:51:44,020
Maybe I'll give you my take first.

1050
00:51:44,040 --> 00:51:44,560
No, no, no, no, no.

1051
00:51:44,880 --> 00:51:45,140
Okay.

1052
00:51:45,560 --> 00:51:46,000
That's true.

1053
00:51:46,100 --> 00:51:46,240
All right.

1054
00:51:46,260 --> 00:51:46,660
No, you're right.

1055
00:51:46,700 --> 00:51:46,920
You're right.

1056
00:51:46,960 --> 00:51:47,140
You're right.

1057
00:51:47,180 --> 00:51:48,560
I've been asking the questions this whole time.

1058
00:51:48,620 --> 00:51:49,140
Give me your take.

1059
00:51:49,140 --> 00:51:53,020
I don't think CVSS is very useful.

1060
00:51:53,960 --> 00:51:54,600
That's it.

1061
00:51:55,160 --> 00:51:55,400
Okay.

1062
00:51:55,580 --> 00:51:59,640
Like, it is a factor that you can consider.

1063
00:51:59,960 --> 00:52:02,360
I don't think it should be the only factor you consider.

1064
00:52:02,540 --> 00:52:04,080
And I don't think it's a perfect system.

1065
00:52:04,300 --> 00:52:14,760
I don't think any system like this, whether you want to be VISS, whether you want to be CVSS, whether you want to be ABCD.

1066
00:52:14,760 --> 00:52:18,760
I don't think it's a flying...

1067
00:52:18,760 --> 00:52:19,180
You know.

1068
00:52:19,280 --> 00:52:20,420
I don't think...

1069
00:52:20,420 --> 00:52:21,180
Okay.

1070
00:52:21,880 --> 00:52:22,860
Fill in the blank, right?

1071
00:52:22,940 --> 00:52:38,920
Like, all of these systems are trying to solve a problem that is incredibly difficult to solve, which is that you're trying to take a case-by-case scenario that is truly impact-dependent and generify it into a set of factors that you can apply globally.

1072
00:52:39,180 --> 00:52:41,600
And that just doesn't really work.

1073
00:52:41,600 --> 00:52:49,420
Like, we've had some really interesting discussions, me and you, with programs about this.

1074
00:52:50,140 --> 00:52:57,080
And, like, from an optimization perspective, every program wants this because it makes payouts easier.

1075
00:52:57,240 --> 00:52:58,420
It makes payouts more consistent.

1076
00:52:58,680 --> 00:53:00,600
It makes processing bugs and handling...

1077
00:53:00,600 --> 00:53:01,240
Streamlines everything.

1078
00:53:01,440 --> 00:53:02,480
It streamlines everything.

1079
00:53:02,720 --> 00:53:02,900
Yeah.

1080
00:53:03,060 --> 00:53:03,300
Okay.

1081
00:53:03,320 --> 00:53:03,820
That's great.

1082
00:53:03,940 --> 00:53:06,580
However, you can't do that.

1083
00:53:06,580 --> 00:53:13,100
Like, you can't just say, like, oh, if it checks these boxes, then it is this bounty or it is this bad.

1084
00:53:13,180 --> 00:53:17,280
Because some cases, yes, you can do that.

1085
00:53:17,980 --> 00:53:19,320
Many cases, you cannot.

1086
00:53:19,320 --> 00:53:19,960
Okay.

1087
00:53:19,960 --> 00:53:24,020
So, lots to unpack there.

1088
00:53:24,200 --> 00:53:27,860
Lots of, you know, coming up with a problem and not coming up with a solution.

1089
00:53:28,880 --> 00:53:41,920
And I'm actually going to take a little bit of a controversial position here that I am slightly truly believing, which is that CVSS is, like...

1090
00:53:41,920 --> 00:53:46,580
Like, the programs that really stick to CVSS, they...

1091
00:53:46,580 --> 00:53:47,520
It works well.

1092
00:53:47,840 --> 00:53:48,100
You know?

1093
00:53:48,200 --> 00:53:59,520
Like, if you look at Shopify and you look at PayPal and some of the other ones that really stick to it like glue, I think that rarely are the...

1094
00:53:59,520 --> 00:54:04,340
And maybe it's just because those programs have really good bounties, which is also another thing, right?

1095
00:54:04,400 --> 00:54:05,840
Those programs pay very high bounties.

1096
00:54:05,840 --> 00:54:15,100
So, you know, you could get your bug rated at a medium 6.8 and still be walking out there with, you know, well into the four figures, you know, range.

1097
00:54:16,760 --> 00:54:25,040
But, yeah, I think there doesn't necessarily have to be a system that makes it all streamlined and stuff.

1098
00:54:25,100 --> 00:54:33,220
But having some sort of baseline for an attacker to know, and maybe this is just a piece in your policy where it's like, hey, we really prioritize user data.

1099
00:54:33,220 --> 00:54:36,920
And if you can get access to user data, we are very, very interested in that.

1100
00:54:37,040 --> 00:54:41,480
Or we don't really care as much about user data as we care about, like, you getting access to our source code.

1101
00:54:41,540 --> 00:54:44,160
And if you can get access to our source code, you know, very bad.

1102
00:54:44,840 --> 00:54:48,260
That sort of direction is really helpful for me in understanding the threat model of a company.

1103
00:54:48,960 --> 00:54:49,080
Yeah.

1104
00:54:49,220 --> 00:54:50,680
No, I think that's a great point.

1105
00:54:50,800 --> 00:54:55,680
Like, again, generally I err towards trying to pay...

1106
00:54:55,680 --> 00:54:58,000
Like, I think programs should be paying by impact.

1107
00:54:58,300 --> 00:55:01,440
Like, I think that impact is really what matters.

1108
00:55:01,440 --> 00:55:04,520
It's not about, like, I got an XSS.

1109
00:55:04,940 --> 00:55:05,580
That's great.

1110
00:55:05,720 --> 00:55:06,920
What can you do with it?

1111
00:55:07,060 --> 00:55:09,220
Like, can you steal my data?

1112
00:55:09,360 --> 00:55:10,660
Can you take over my account?

1113
00:55:11,000 --> 00:55:13,280
Can you only pop an alert?

1114
00:55:13,600 --> 00:55:15,780
Like, what is the actual impact of this?

1115
00:55:15,840 --> 00:55:21,320
Because if it's nothing, then why am I paying, you know, a flat bounty for this?

1116
00:55:21,340 --> 00:55:26,500
Or why am I paying what I paid for the last XSS that could do an account takeover on this XSS, which can do nothing?

1117
00:55:27,020 --> 00:55:27,120
Right?

1118
00:55:27,240 --> 00:55:27,380
Right.

1119
00:55:27,380 --> 00:55:30,960
And I think that type of information is really important.

1120
00:55:31,340 --> 00:55:35,400
And what you said about, like, if you really stick to CVSS, then it works well.

1121
00:55:35,580 --> 00:55:38,740
I think it works well in the sense that you have a consistent program.

1122
00:55:39,060 --> 00:55:39,220
Right?

1123
00:55:39,340 --> 00:55:42,260
So what that means is that, one, your process is streamlined.

1124
00:55:42,400 --> 00:55:44,020
And two, your process is consistent.

1125
00:55:44,020 --> 00:55:48,980
So an XSS is going to grade the same, is going to pay the same all the time.

1126
00:55:48,980 --> 00:55:57,660
And if that's how you want to handle your security program and you say, we've got this much money for bug bounty and we're just going to, you know, we're going to pay 5K for every XSS.

1127
00:55:57,880 --> 00:55:59,780
We're going to pay 2K for every subdomain takeover.

1128
00:56:00,120 --> 00:56:00,860
That's fine.

1129
00:56:01,180 --> 00:56:10,560
But you have to, like, budget and account for that and just be willing to, regardless of the impact, you have to be willing to press that button and pay the bounty at the amount.

1130
00:56:10,880 --> 00:56:11,020
Right?

1131
00:56:11,260 --> 00:56:11,460
Yeah.

1132
00:56:11,460 --> 00:56:16,280
And a lot of programs, I think, don't look at it that way, which is fair.

1133
00:56:16,580 --> 00:56:18,880
Like, I don't look at it that way.

1134
00:56:18,880 --> 00:56:19,860
Like, we don't use CVSS.

1135
00:56:20,180 --> 00:56:24,740
And I don't think CVSS is a great solution because of what I said.

1136
00:56:24,820 --> 00:56:26,640
Like, there's so much nuance in impact.

1137
00:56:27,060 --> 00:56:30,880
And so what you pointed out about, like, what does the program care about?

1138
00:56:30,940 --> 00:56:34,740
This is something that I see almost no programs do well.

1139
00:56:34,740 --> 00:56:42,080
And, like, this is something that I think should be addressed from a – probably from a platform perspective as well.

1140
00:56:42,600 --> 00:56:48,800
But there needs to be a better way for programs to explain to researchers what their security threat model looks like.

1141
00:56:49,780 --> 00:56:56,280
And what that means is, as a security team, when you report a bug to me, how do I think about it?

1142
00:56:56,700 --> 00:56:58,320
What are the things that I'm looking for?

1143
00:56:58,540 --> 00:56:59,920
How am I grading the impact?

1144
00:56:59,920 --> 00:57:00,320
Amen.

1145
00:57:00,320 --> 00:57:07,340
And so there's not a great way to communicate that from the program side to the researcher side.

1146
00:57:07,780 --> 00:57:13,240
And especially for programs that don't use CVSS, there's, like, basically zero guidance.

1147
00:57:13,420 --> 00:57:13,480
Right?

1148
00:57:13,560 --> 00:57:17,480
And so those are the programs where you submit something and you're like, this is a crit for sure.

1149
00:57:17,580 --> 00:57:19,180
And then they regrade it to a medium.

1150
00:57:19,420 --> 00:57:19,540
Be low.

1151
00:57:19,740 --> 00:57:22,820
And, like, you know, you're like, whoa, what just happened?

1152
00:57:23,040 --> 00:57:23,240
Right?

1153
00:57:23,960 --> 00:57:29,580
And oftentimes it may be one of, you know, the early reports to that program that you've, you know, you're still trying to feel it out.

1154
00:57:29,580 --> 00:57:35,800
And that takes literal, like, multiple reports to start to feel out manually what does the program care about?

1155
00:57:35,900 --> 00:57:37,340
How do they respond to this type of stuff?

1156
00:57:37,640 --> 00:57:38,940
Like, do they care about this?

1157
00:57:38,980 --> 00:57:40,000
Do they not care about this?

1158
00:57:40,080 --> 00:57:45,440
Like, understanding that stuff in a trial and error method really sucks from a researcher perspective.

1159
00:57:46,120 --> 00:57:51,520
And so I think programs could definitely do a better job in saying we care about user data.

1160
00:57:51,520 --> 00:57:55,180
Specifically, even maybe we care about these user data fields.

1161
00:57:55,420 --> 00:58:03,320
Now, that second question right there, you'll have programs who will immediately say no to that just because of legal and comms.

1162
00:58:03,800 --> 00:58:06,860
Like, they don't want to point out explicitly.

1163
00:58:06,860 --> 00:58:09,500
They don't want to put targets on specific stuff.

1164
00:58:09,760 --> 00:58:09,840
Yeah.

1165
00:58:09,980 --> 00:58:10,120
Right.

1166
00:58:10,300 --> 00:58:11,420
And so, like, I get that.

1167
00:58:11,520 --> 00:58:12,720
But, like, that's fine.

1168
00:58:12,780 --> 00:58:13,900
Like, you don't have to go that far.

1169
00:58:13,900 --> 00:58:19,500
Just say, like, we care about user data and we would care if you can do, you know, X, Y, and Z.

1170
00:58:20,040 --> 00:58:24,880
The other side that I see, by the way, is, like, way too extreme in terms of examples.

1171
00:58:25,220 --> 00:58:32,880
Well, where, like, I've seen a crypto program, for example, where they will say, we consider it a crit if.

1172
00:58:32,880 --> 00:58:43,280
And then they have four bullet points with very specific scenarios laid out of, like, detailed, like, oh, you can execute, like, X, Y, and Z through, like, a specific.

1173
00:58:43,500 --> 00:58:43,760
I'm like.

1174
00:58:43,880 --> 00:58:44,020
Right.

1175
00:58:44,540 --> 00:58:48,960
This function never returns a negative number, then we're screwed, you know?

1176
00:58:49,140 --> 00:58:53,960
Well, I'm just going to go test this one specific use case that I know your security team already knows is invulnerable.

1177
00:58:54,160 --> 00:58:54,920
Like, you know what I mean?

1178
00:58:54,940 --> 00:58:56,260
Like, that's not helpful either.

1179
00:58:56,260 --> 00:59:05,680
So, I think, like, being more generic about, like, the things that you care about, not the specific scenarios that you care about would definitely help.

1180
00:59:06,240 --> 00:59:06,480
For sure.

1181
00:59:06,500 --> 00:59:08,060
And then somehow figuring out.

1182
00:59:08,140 --> 00:59:16,180
I don't have a great solution for this either, but somehow figuring out some way to explain, like, the way that your security team is thinking about bugs when they come in.

1183
00:59:17,260 --> 00:59:17,720
Whether that be.

1184
00:59:17,740 --> 00:59:18,020
Well, Dangel.

1185
00:59:18,240 --> 00:59:19,100
Is it on the main domain?

1186
00:59:19,780 --> 00:59:21,780
Does it have access to cookies?

1187
00:59:21,980 --> 00:59:22,160
Yeah.

1188
00:59:22,560 --> 00:59:23,800
Can it talk with the main domain?

1189
00:59:23,800 --> 00:59:26,740
Are there ever cookies scoped to this?

1190
00:59:26,820 --> 00:59:29,260
Like, some of these things you can figure out as a researcher.

1191
00:59:29,380 --> 00:59:32,200
Some of these things the program needs to provide.

1192
00:59:32,660 --> 00:59:32,820
Dude.

1193
00:59:33,580 --> 00:59:35,620
Like, that was great.

1194
00:59:35,900 --> 00:59:43,440
And I think we need to start a critical thinking bug bounty program consultancy based off of this conversation.

1195
00:59:43,440 --> 00:59:45,520
Because there's a lot of high quality stuff in there.

1196
00:59:45,520 --> 00:59:52,640
And I think a lot of lessons you've learned as being on the program side and on the hacker side in extreme ways on both, right?

1197
00:59:52,640 --> 00:59:58,120
Managing some of the biggest bug bounty programs and also, or contributing to some of the biggest bug bounty programs.

1198
00:59:58,120 --> 01:00:03,940
And also hacking on, you know, the live hacking events in the, you know, cream of the crop scene there.

1199
01:00:03,940 --> 01:00:05,840
So, that's really cool.

1200
01:00:06,000 --> 01:00:10,640
And I think CVSS definitely has its flaws.

1201
01:00:10,800 --> 01:00:13,880
But for me, that consistency that you mentioned, that's kind of what I want to see.

1202
01:00:14,240 --> 01:00:21,900
Because if I can see the consistency, if I know what the system is, then I can work within the system, right?

1203
01:00:21,940 --> 01:00:23,940
That's our whole thing as hackers.

1204
01:00:23,940 --> 01:00:28,520
Is working within the system and making our goals get accomplished, right?

1205
01:00:28,660 --> 01:00:31,020
And we talked about this a little bit with Inti.

1206
01:00:31,240 --> 01:00:37,660
But, like, the ethical boundaries of sort of gaming the vulnerability, you know, rating system.

1207
01:00:38,640 --> 01:00:47,280
But at the end of the day, it reflects, it truly, if the security team's priorities are truly aligned with their vulnerability rating system, then it should be a dub for all players.

1208
01:00:47,280 --> 01:00:57,320
Yeah, and, like, I'm definitely winging this take right here, which is that I think CVSS isn't the worst place to, like, start.

1209
01:00:58,120 --> 01:01:00,140
And what I mean by that is, like, calculate the CVSS.

1210
01:01:00,500 --> 01:01:05,520
And then if that doesn't line up with your security team's threat model, try to figure out why.

1211
01:01:06,220 --> 01:01:09,780
And those are probably your mitigating factors, right?

1212
01:01:10,100 --> 01:01:15,840
And what I mean by that is, like, okay, if something is a crit, but it's actually a medium because blank.

1213
01:01:15,840 --> 01:01:18,180
Like, well, that's why, right?

1214
01:01:18,460 --> 01:01:21,200
CVSS can't really account for that easily, right?

1215
01:01:21,260 --> 01:01:23,660
Yeah, maybe it's, like, attack complexity high or something.

1216
01:01:23,820 --> 01:01:26,800
But, like, it's, you know, it's not, right?

1217
01:01:26,860 --> 01:01:34,700
Like, again, like, if you have an XSS that can do nothing versus an XSS that can do an account takeover, those are two very, very different things.

1218
01:01:35,260 --> 01:01:44,380
And attack complexity or privileges required or whatever, like, those don't really account for what's probably causing that change in severity, right?

1219
01:01:44,380 --> 01:01:57,180
And so I think, again, being transparent with the researcher, describing what has changed that has caused it to not be a critical, but to be actually a medium or whatever, is really, really important for the program perspective.

1220
01:01:57,180 --> 01:02:04,300
And to help build those relationships that lead to fruitful security research from your hacker base, right?

1221
01:02:04,520 --> 01:02:04,620
Yeah.

1222
01:02:05,240 --> 01:02:05,440
Yeah.

1223
01:02:06,360 --> 01:02:07,360
Good stuff, man.

1224
01:02:08,500 --> 01:02:08,980
Whew.

1225
01:02:09,140 --> 01:02:11,700
We are an hour and one minute in.

1226
01:02:11,900 --> 01:02:14,180
I've had a little bit of a draining day.

1227
01:02:14,300 --> 01:02:18,560
So that steam, that steam that was, you know, coming out earlier.

1228
01:02:18,900 --> 01:02:19,520
I'm still going.

1229
01:02:19,740 --> 01:02:22,280
Just read them as you wrote them.

1230
01:02:22,280 --> 01:02:22,720
Yeah.

1231
01:02:23,080 --> 01:02:23,500
All right.

1232
01:02:23,600 --> 01:02:24,120
Well, okay.

1233
01:02:24,120 --> 01:02:25,420
I'll just read them as I wrote them.

1234
01:02:26,560 --> 01:02:28,980
Let me just hit you with this one, big boy.

1235
01:02:31,460 --> 01:02:34,280
Regarding bounty payments and budgets, it's not your money.

1236
01:02:34,560 --> 01:02:36,320
So why are you so freaking stingy with it?

1237
01:02:36,800 --> 01:02:37,000
Yeah.

1238
01:02:37,160 --> 01:02:37,380
Okay.

1239
01:02:37,500 --> 01:02:42,920
So as I mentioned, bounties are, sorry, budgets are very complex topics.

1240
01:02:43,100 --> 01:02:43,400
Okay.

1241
01:02:46,600 --> 01:02:48,380
Let's see how generic I can talk about this.

1242
01:02:48,380 --> 01:02:56,740
Essentially, you have to imagine a budget for a company as a giant pool of money that starts from the very, very top of that company.

1243
01:02:57,280 --> 01:03:02,760
And every single layer of management you go down, you chop another piece off of it.

1244
01:03:02,960 --> 01:03:03,220
Okay.

1245
01:03:04,600 --> 01:03:04,920
And so.

1246
01:03:04,920 --> 01:03:09,140
So as soon as he said giant pool of money, I just like, the eyes went big.

1247
01:03:09,140 --> 01:03:11,540
Like, I'm like, yeah, that, that's what I want.

1248
01:03:12,160 --> 01:03:12,560
Yeah.

1249
01:03:12,640 --> 01:03:12,840
Right.

1250
01:03:13,460 --> 01:03:16,240
Justin wants the entire, he'll just take the entire budget.

1251
01:03:16,460 --> 01:03:17,320
I'll take that.

1252
01:03:17,320 --> 01:03:18,200
No, I'm sorry.

1253
01:03:18,320 --> 01:03:18,580
Go ahead.

1254
01:03:19,360 --> 01:03:23,140
And so the reality is that like money is complicated.

1255
01:03:23,700 --> 01:03:31,700
And when you make a payment, like I said, out of a bug bounty program that comes out of the bug bounty budget pool,

1256
01:03:31,700 --> 01:03:38,360
which is part of the greater security team budget, which is part of the probably engineering team budget,

1257
01:03:38,480 --> 01:03:41,620
which is part of the yearly budget for the entire company.

1258
01:03:41,620 --> 01:03:42,620
Right.

1259
01:03:42,620 --> 01:03:50,360
And when anything starts to shift, whether you go over or under that, then lots of other factors come involved.

1260
01:03:50,580 --> 01:03:50,700
Right.

1261
01:03:50,820 --> 01:03:52,480
It's like, oh, there's extra money here.

1262
01:03:52,620 --> 01:03:54,780
Let's take that and spend it on a security tool.

1263
01:03:54,780 --> 01:03:59,500
Or let's, oh, you don't, you didn't use all that money.

1264
01:03:59,620 --> 01:03:59,840
All right.

1265
01:03:59,880 --> 01:04:02,380
Well, that other team is going to get more money because they needed it last year.

1266
01:04:02,860 --> 01:04:12,380
And so there's like all sorts of like weird meta aspects to paying a bounty and to where that money is coming from.

1267
01:04:12,380 --> 01:04:18,280
That, yes, it's not your money, but it kind of is your money because it's your team's money.

1268
01:04:18,620 --> 01:04:25,740
It's, you know, it's the money that you have for next year or it's the tooling that you're planning to buy in six months.

1269
01:04:25,740 --> 01:04:30,300
Or like there are a lot of other aspects where it kind of is your money.

1270
01:04:30,540 --> 01:04:35,800
It's your money in the sense that you have like ownership over it, but it's not your money.

1271
01:04:36,160 --> 01:04:36,260
Right.

1272
01:04:36,820 --> 01:04:37,220
Yeah.

1273
01:04:37,220 --> 01:04:39,200
And so I think that's where.

1274
01:04:39,220 --> 01:04:40,120
You're a steward of that money.

1275
01:04:40,420 --> 01:04:45,240
You're supposed to be using this money for the greater purpose of the organization that you're working with.

1276
01:04:45,560 --> 01:04:45,980
Makes sense.

1277
01:04:46,120 --> 01:04:46,240
Yeah.

1278
01:04:46,240 --> 01:04:54,520
And I think very few companies are able to keep up with the type of pace that gets set by live hacking event programs specifically.

1279
01:04:55,640 --> 01:05:04,460
I think it really sets an unfair bar in terms of what people should be paying and what's considered a reasonable bounty.

1280
01:05:04,460 --> 01:05:08,080
Those are just blank checks sometimes too, you know, to the security team.

1281
01:05:08,460 --> 01:05:08,840
So, yeah.

1282
01:05:08,860 --> 01:05:09,000
Yeah.

1283
01:05:09,120 --> 01:05:14,520
I mean, it's like, you know, you see a program that pays multiple millions of dollars at a single event.

1284
01:05:15,040 --> 01:05:21,400
That is many times the full security budget for plenty of companies.

1285
01:05:21,400 --> 01:05:26,640
And like the reality is that not every company can do that.

1286
01:05:26,720 --> 01:05:28,220
Not every company can keep up with that.

1287
01:05:28,380 --> 01:05:34,000
And so that is also part of where the stinginess comes in, which is like, is this something that we actually care about?

1288
01:05:34,000 --> 01:05:40,560
Like if I'm looking at my budget and what I'm supposed to be paying for this, is this something that's worth paying?

1289
01:05:41,320 --> 01:05:43,480
You know, say I have $50,000 in my budget.

1290
01:05:43,600 --> 01:05:47,140
Is this worth paying 20% for a 10K crit?

1291
01:05:47,520 --> 01:05:47,920
Right.

1292
01:05:48,220 --> 01:05:56,300
Of my whole budget for who knows how, like, you know, what if it's February and you got 50K in your budget and you just got a 10K crit?

1293
01:05:56,320 --> 01:05:57,620
You got 50K in your budget.

1294
01:05:57,860 --> 01:05:58,340
I don't know.

1295
01:05:58,340 --> 01:06:03,180
And it's like, oh, okay, I'm two months in and I just blew 20% of my budget.

1296
01:06:03,520 --> 01:06:07,640
Like what do I, you know, what happens if this happens again in two weeks?

1297
01:06:07,840 --> 01:06:08,280
Right.

1298
01:06:08,300 --> 01:06:17,540
And so I think there's a lot of aspects where programs start to get stingy because there are other aspects involved around like where's the money going to come from?

1299
01:06:17,820 --> 01:06:19,680
And how's the company doing in general?

1300
01:06:20,080 --> 01:06:20,380
Yeah.

1301
01:06:20,400 --> 01:06:22,480
I mean, there's tons of other things.

1302
01:06:22,480 --> 01:06:31,980
But yeah, a lot of it is just really comes down to budgets are weird and confusing and getting more money or having extra money is like a weird balance.

1303
01:06:32,300 --> 01:06:34,080
And every company kind of handles that differently.

1304
01:06:34,400 --> 01:06:37,640
A lot of times it's not received well in either case.

1305
01:06:38,500 --> 01:06:47,260
But on the flip side of that, you know, the more bounties you pay, the more budget you may get allocated the next year.

1306
01:06:48,020 --> 01:06:48,660
Yeah, man.

1307
01:06:48,800 --> 01:06:50,580
I have a really...

1308
01:06:50,580 --> 01:06:51,840
I can really help you out here.

1309
01:06:51,840 --> 01:06:56,800
You know, like I could submit, you could pay them really good and then you'll get more money next year.

1310
01:06:56,800 --> 01:07:06,100
This is not going to be a popular take, but I think that programs that pay a lot of money are really just messing it all up.

1311
01:07:06,560 --> 01:07:06,780
What?

1312
01:07:06,920 --> 01:07:08,340
Like, here's the thing.

1313
01:07:08,540 --> 01:07:08,980
Here's the thing.

1314
01:07:09,280 --> 01:07:09,460
Okay.

1315
01:07:09,600 --> 01:07:13,060
As a bug bounty researcher, would I like to get lots of bounties?

1316
01:07:13,460 --> 01:07:13,860
Absolutely.

1317
01:07:14,080 --> 01:07:14,900
Every day of the week.

1318
01:07:14,900 --> 01:07:34,900
As a program, would I want to be paying millions of dollars every single year because my security team is not finding those things and not fixing those things and not securing from the inside, but putting all this emphasis in money.

1319
01:07:34,900 --> 01:07:44,500
Literally just like spending money because we're paying other researchers to find these things that our team should be proactively trying to fix and find.

1320
01:07:44,660 --> 01:07:45,460
I don't know, man.

1321
01:07:45,640 --> 01:07:46,900
I think...

1322
01:07:46,900 --> 01:07:50,160
And I know you said it's a hot take or whatever, and it is.

1323
01:07:50,380 --> 01:07:51,080
Oh, for sure.

1324
01:07:51,140 --> 01:07:51,880
It's a hot take.

1325
01:07:51,880 --> 01:07:54,380
But let's say this, okay?

1326
01:07:54,720 --> 01:07:57,080
Let's say you spend $500 or...

1327
01:07:57,080 --> 01:07:57,780
Not $500.

1328
01:07:58,180 --> 01:08:00,880
$500,000 on your security...

1329
01:08:00,880 --> 01:08:01,040
That's a big difference.

1330
01:08:01,420 --> 01:08:01,660
I know.

1331
01:08:01,760 --> 01:08:02,180
I'm sorry.

1332
01:08:02,920 --> 01:08:09,040
$500,000 on your bug bounty reports for this year, okay?

1333
01:08:11,840 --> 01:08:13,060
Let's say what?

1334
01:08:13,180 --> 01:08:15,620
Average bounty of 2K.

1335
01:08:16,260 --> 01:08:21,780
So you're resolving 250 vulnerabilities there, roughly, right?

1336
01:08:22,880 --> 01:08:31,820
If you were to hire an employee, let's say a Silicon Valley engineer, AppSec, you know, employee, right?

1337
01:08:32,220 --> 01:08:33,840
Let's say you can hire...

1338
01:08:33,840 --> 01:08:39,780
Let's be generous, and let's say we can hire two of them for $500,000, which I don't think you can.

1339
01:08:40,780 --> 01:08:47,940
What are the chances that they will produce 125 valid vulnerabilities each in one year?

1340
01:08:48,600 --> 01:08:50,000
On some of the hard...

1341
01:08:50,000 --> 01:08:52,220
You know, some very hard in scope.

1342
01:08:52,560 --> 01:08:54,100
I feel like that's pretty unlikely.

1343
01:08:55,080 --> 01:09:00,260
For the math here, by the way, 52 weeks, five days a week is 250 days a year.

1344
01:09:00,440 --> 01:09:02,820
So you're asking for a bug every other day.

1345
01:09:02,820 --> 01:09:03,260
Okay.

1346
01:09:06,000 --> 01:09:11,020
Well, no one's working 52 weeks a year, though, as a Silicon Valley engineer, you know, just to be clear.

1347
01:09:11,340 --> 01:09:11,900
Sure, sure.

1348
01:09:12,080 --> 01:09:13,600
But, you know, they're also not...

1349
01:09:13,600 --> 01:09:14,580
They might find two bugs in a day.

1350
01:09:14,580 --> 01:09:15,680
Maybe 50, but yeah.

1351
01:09:15,680 --> 01:09:16,080
Yeah, whatever.

1352
01:09:16,240 --> 01:09:20,400
Like, I think the reality is that the break-even is pretty close.

1353
01:09:23,660 --> 01:09:25,360
But if you...

1354
01:09:25,360 --> 01:09:34,880
Again, like, I defer back to the fact that if you have a program that is consistently paying insane amounts of bounties and that number's not going down,

1355
01:09:35,180 --> 01:09:42,140
that means either you're raising your bounties and finding less or you're not doing your job.

1356
01:09:43,400 --> 01:09:44,420
That's how I see it.

1357
01:09:44,420 --> 01:09:46,300
Like, you're just not...

1358
01:09:46,300 --> 01:09:49,420
Like, a security team should be paying less bounties, right?

1359
01:09:49,500 --> 01:09:57,100
Like, I don't understand how your engineering team could be outpacing security so badly that you're paying more money.

1360
01:09:58,880 --> 01:10:01,500
And also, I don't see how that's a good thing.

1361
01:10:01,840 --> 01:10:02,820
It's kind of based, though.

1362
01:10:02,880 --> 01:10:03,460
Not going to lie.

1363
01:10:03,940 --> 01:10:14,400
Like, you know, like, at the end of the day, you know, from a business perspective, unless you're, you know, growing extremely fast,

1364
01:10:14,420 --> 01:10:21,340
and the number of engineers that you're employing to write code that have mistakes in it is increasing very fast,

1365
01:10:21,680 --> 01:10:26,000
over time, security is a cost, you know?

1366
01:10:26,260 --> 01:10:32,880
And that cost ideally should be going down as you understand what mistakes your company is making over time.

1367
01:10:32,920 --> 01:10:33,180
Yeah.

1368
01:10:33,180 --> 01:10:34,020
Or it goes up because of the value.

1369
01:10:34,020 --> 01:10:35,520
I don't like it, but I kind of agree with you.

1370
01:10:35,620 --> 01:10:35,760
Yeah.

1371
01:10:35,900 --> 01:10:36,020
Yeah.

1372
01:10:36,060 --> 01:10:42,140
Like, I just don't think that, like, I see so many programs that are, like, boasting, like, oh, we paid so much money this year.

1373
01:10:42,320 --> 01:10:43,460
Yeah, man, that's awesome.

1374
01:10:43,580 --> 01:10:45,280
But, like, why?

1375
01:10:45,280 --> 01:10:49,440
Like, what is happening internally?

1376
01:10:49,640 --> 01:10:51,300
Like, what is your security team doing?

1377
01:10:51,540 --> 01:10:54,660
Because they should be helping, like, cut this back so much.

1378
01:10:54,880 --> 01:10:56,160
Let me devil's advocate that, though.

1379
01:10:56,300 --> 01:11:01,860
The devil's advocate of that is that you're resolving actual vulnerabilities.

1380
01:11:02,240 --> 01:11:03,780
And you're finding out these problems.

1381
01:11:03,920 --> 01:11:08,360
Every single problem that is being reported and paid for is a valid vulnerability.

1382
01:11:08,600 --> 01:11:10,800
And that's the beauty of bug bounty, right?

1383
01:11:10,800 --> 01:11:17,420
So if you're paying out more money, it doesn't – I guess you could try to trace it all the way back down to the cost.

1384
01:11:17,860 --> 01:11:20,620
But at the point you're paying it out, it's already a sunken cost.

1385
01:11:20,960 --> 01:11:23,140
You already have that vulnerability on your platform.

1386
01:11:23,260 --> 01:11:30,540
And at any given point, if you could pay the money to resolve the vulnerability, that's the choice you're making because of risk, right?

1387
01:11:30,540 --> 01:11:30,940
Right.

1388
01:11:32,040 --> 01:11:47,500
Yes, but if the engineer finds it – like, if your security team finds that and fixes it and then also simultaneously fixes 10 other bugs, not only have they saved you so much more money, but also that's what they're getting paid to do.

1389
01:11:48,020 --> 01:11:48,180
You know?

1390
01:11:48,240 --> 01:11:59,520
Like, on top of paying bounties, you're paying security engineers to run the security team, maintain the program, like, do day-to-day stuff, and also you're paying bounties, right?

1391
01:11:59,520 --> 01:12:13,040
Like, imagine if you could take the $7 million that you pay in your bounties and spend one and a half on salaries and then keep $6 million to do whatever you – like, I don't know.

1392
01:12:13,180 --> 01:12:26,540
Like, it's just a really hard concept for me to understand why when I see companies bragging about the fact that they're paying super large amount of bounties every year, I'm like, yeah, maybe for like a year or two, that's a good thing to see.

1393
01:12:26,540 --> 01:12:32,760
But after that, you should be paying nowhere even close to that because your security team should be learning from this.

1394
01:12:33,040 --> 01:12:34,460
They should be making changes.

1395
01:12:34,660 --> 01:12:39,580
They should be putting stuff in place that's going to – like, oh, this happened because X, Y, Z.

1396
01:12:39,920 --> 01:12:42,820
Let's figure out how to detect that so it doesn't happen again.

1397
01:12:43,080 --> 01:12:43,260
Yeah.

1398
01:12:43,720 --> 01:12:47,180
Like, this is what – from a security team perspective, this is what we do.

1399
01:12:47,600 --> 01:12:50,940
And when I see companies who are like, oh, we paid $5 million last year.

1400
01:12:51,100 --> 01:12:53,080
Oh, before that, we paid $2 million.

1401
01:12:53,080 --> 01:12:54,740
I'm like, what?

1402
01:12:55,420 --> 01:12:55,780
Yeah.

1403
01:12:56,720 --> 01:12:57,080
Yeah.

1404
01:12:58,540 --> 01:12:59,260
I see it.

1405
01:12:59,340 --> 01:12:59,820
I see it.

1406
01:12:59,880 --> 01:13:01,260
I don't love it, but I see it.

1407
01:13:01,620 --> 01:13:02,620
And it sort of makes sense.

1408
01:13:02,720 --> 01:13:07,960
And I think that is one of the things that's tricky about BookMountie as well is you've got to study the bugs you're getting.

1409
01:13:08,860 --> 01:13:16,200
And you really, to be honest, you need at least, you know, maybe depending on the volume of your program.

1410
01:13:16,200 --> 01:13:23,640
But a lot of programs need a dedicated asset, a dedicated person to that because it is so much research.

1411
01:13:23,800 --> 01:13:32,840
And every single time, you know, you look at a bug bounty report, you're not seeing all of the layers of research that went into getting that bug bounty report out there.

1412
01:13:32,840 --> 01:13:36,540
So there's a lot of reverse engineering that needs to happen from the program side.

1413
01:13:36,540 --> 01:13:40,240
And I was privy to a conversation lately.

1414
01:13:40,480 --> 01:13:54,120
Thankfully, somebody came up to me and told me about how one of my reports was dealt with within their internal organization where after I submitted this report, their internal red team is actually, you know, sort of weaponizing these reports.

1415
01:13:54,120 --> 01:14:01,900
And they were able to use them to accomplish, you know, a couple tasks, a couple objectives that they had for the red team goals.

1416
01:14:03,040 --> 01:14:05,260
And that was really that was really cool to hear.

